1
00:00:00,108 --> 00:00:04,538
Hello everyone and welcome to
my talk, and let's get started.

2
00:00:04,628 --> 00:00:08,588
Your monitoring is green and
your customers can't pay you.

3
00:00:09,017 --> 00:00:12,537
That's the whole talk, and I'll
spend the next few minutes on why

4
00:00:12,538 --> 00:00:14,967
it happens and what to do about it.

5
00:00:15,127 --> 00:00:16,027
I'm Eleftheria.

6
00:00:16,058 --> 00:00:20,717
I'm a developer advocate at Uptime
Robot, which is an uptime monitoring

7
00:00:20,717 --> 00:00:25,567
service that over 3.4 million people
use to watch their websites, APIs,

8
00:00:26,058 --> 00:00:27,977
and services from the outside.

9
00:00:28,097 --> 00:00:32,848
Before that, I was a developer, so
I've been on both sides of this, the

10
00:00:32,887 --> 00:00:37,957
one setting up the checks and the
one getting the alert at 2:00 in the

11
00:00:37,967 --> 00:00:40,667
morning, or worse, not getting it.

12
00:00:40,727 --> 00:00:43,287
Here's the thing I want you to leave with.

13
00:00:43,328 --> 00:00:46,938
Most outages today don't
happen in the code you wrote.

14
00:00:47,317 --> 00:00:51,957
They happen in something your code
depends on, and most monitoring

15
00:00:51,977 --> 00:00:56,397
setups are built to watch the first
thing and completely miss the second.

16
00:00:56,938 --> 00:00:58,847
Let me show you what that looks like.

17
00:00:58,918 --> 00:01:01,507
The 20 minutes nobody noticed.

18
00:01:02,078 --> 00:01:06,357
It's a Thursday afternoon, and
your dashboard looks like this.

19
00:01:06,398 --> 00:01:09,228
Every check you configured
comes back healthy.

20
00:01:09,667 --> 00:01:10,467
Homepage, 200, API, 200, login page,

21
00:01:11,467 --> 00:01:13,808
200. Response times are normal.

22
00:01:13,878 --> 00:01:18,117
Nobody is on a call in a way that
matters because nothing is wrong.

23
00:01:18,268 --> 00:01:20,567
At 2:00 PM, a support ticket arrives.

24
00:01:20,677 --> 00:01:24,608
A customer says the checkout
button doesn't do anything.

25
00:01:25,078 --> 00:01:29,208
The support person tries it,
sees a spinner, and assumes

26
00:01:29,217 --> 00:01:31,257
it is the customer's browser.

27
00:01:31,287 --> 00:01:34,068
At 2:20 PM, there are three tickets.

28
00:01:34,268 --> 00:01:39,698
At 2:30, someone in sales says a demo
just failed in front of a prospect.

29
00:01:39,747 --> 00:01:41,368
An engineer finally looks.

30
00:01:41,397 --> 00:01:43,107
The dashboard is still green.

31
00:01:43,368 --> 00:01:45,237
Every single check is passing.

32
00:01:45,637 --> 00:01:49,818
So they start digging, and 20
minutes later, they find it.

33
00:01:50,008 --> 00:01:53,898
Your payment provider has been
returning errors on a subset of

34
00:01:53,898 --> 00:01:55,948
requests since about an hour ago.

35
00:01:56,637 --> 00:02:01,038
Your app handled it gracefully,
which in practice means it swallowed

36
00:02:01,038 --> 00:02:02,708
the error and showed a spinner.

37
00:02:02,758 --> 00:02:05,677
Your app was up for the entire incident.

38
00:02:05,968 --> 00:02:09,457
Every check you had was
telling the truth, and you lost

39
00:02:09,457 --> 00:02:11,787
roughly 40 minutes of revenue.

40
00:02:12,078 --> 00:02:17,988
Plus a demo, plus a little trust,
and nothing you built was broken.

41
00:02:18,824 --> 00:02:24,054
I use a payment provider in this story
because it's the one everyone recognizes.

42
00:02:24,344 --> 00:02:28,724
Swap in your identity provider,
and it's nobody can log in.

43
00:02:29,084 --> 00:02:33,784
Swap in your SDN, and it's
half your assets don't load.

44
00:02:34,074 --> 00:02:39,044
Swap in the LLM API you added last
quarter, and it's your headline

45
00:02:39,054 --> 00:02:41,224
feature quietly returning nothing.

46
00:02:41,354 --> 00:02:43,894
The thing that broke
isn't the thing you built.

47
00:02:43,924 --> 00:02:46,374
Here is the shape of a modern application.

48
00:02:46,454 --> 00:02:51,164
Your code in the middle and a ring
of things around it that you didn't

49
00:02:51,324 --> 00:02:53,744
write, don't run, and can't fix.

50
00:02:54,054 --> 00:02:59,264
Payments, authentication,
SDN, DNS, transactional email,

51
00:02:59,294 --> 00:03:02,154
increasingly one or more AI APIs.

52
00:03:02,154 --> 00:03:04,694
Each of those is a production dependency.

53
00:03:05,044 --> 00:03:09,224
When any of them degrades, your
product degrades, but there is an

54
00:03:09,224 --> 00:03:11,284
asymmetry that makes this hard.

55
00:03:11,604 --> 00:03:15,394
Your own code, you can read,
test, deploy, and roll back.

56
00:03:15,724 --> 00:03:18,824
A dependency you can only
observe from the outside.

57
00:03:18,914 --> 00:03:21,094
You can't fix Stripe.

58
00:03:21,364 --> 00:03:26,123
You can only find out fast and route
around it or tell your customers.

59
00:03:26,264 --> 00:03:29,804
And dependency failures are
sneaky in a specific way.

60
00:03:29,814 --> 00:03:32,353
They rarely take you fully down.

61
00:03:32,694 --> 00:03:34,653
A hard outage is easy.

62
00:03:34,954 --> 00:03:39,763
Everything returns five hundred,
every alert fires, everybody knows.

63
00:03:40,114 --> 00:03:42,314
A degraded dependency is different.

64
00:03:42,323 --> 00:03:45,654
It's a few percent of requests failing.

65
00:03:45,944 --> 00:03:49,204
It's latency creeping from two
hundred milliseconds to four seconds.

66
00:03:49,293 --> 00:03:54,133
It's a response that
technically valid but empty.

67
00:03:54,444 --> 00:04:00,374
Your app stays up through all of it,
in this sense that the process is

68
00:04:00,374 --> 00:04:02,434
running and the front door opens.

69
00:04:02,993 --> 00:04:05,334
That's why your dashboard stays green.

70
00:04:05,584 --> 00:04:07,463
It's measuring the thing you built.

71
00:04:07,673 --> 00:04:09,314
The thing you built is fine.

72
00:04:09,474 --> 00:04:12,674
Why adding more checks doesn't fix it.

73
00:04:13,253 --> 00:04:18,334
So the natural reaction is, "Fine, I'll
add more checks," and that's the right

74
00:04:18,344 --> 00:04:20,793
instinct pointed in the wrong direction.

75
00:04:21,264 --> 00:04:26,203
If all your new checks still
point at your own endpoint, you

76
00:04:26,204 --> 00:04:28,003
get more of the same signal.

77
00:04:28,573 --> 00:04:30,924
Ten green lights instead of three.

78
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:36,110
You've increased your confidence without
increasing your coverage, and that's worse

79
00:04:36,130 --> 00:04:38,890
because now you trust the dashboard more.

80
00:04:38,930 --> 00:04:42,320
There are three specific ways
a healthy-looking endpoint

81
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:44,560
checks misses a real failure.

82
00:04:45,100 --> 00:04:47,850
The first is the 200 that lies.

83
00:04:47,870 --> 00:04:51,730
Your checkout page returns
200 because the page rendered.

84
00:04:52,100 --> 00:04:57,020
The page rendering has nothing to do
with whether a payment can complete.

85
00:04:57,330 --> 00:05:00,090
A status code tells you
the server answered.

86
00:05:00,110 --> 00:05:02,490
It doesn't tell you the answer was right.

87
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:04,890
The second is the partial failure.

88
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:08,880
Your provider is failing
on 12% of requests.

89
00:05:09,220 --> 00:05:13,620
A check that runs every five minutes
has a decent chance of hitting the

90
00:05:13,650 --> 00:05:19,719
80, 80% that work over and reporting
all clear while a meaningful slice

91
00:05:19,729 --> 00:05:22,809
of your customers can't buy anything.

92
00:05:22,999 --> 00:05:25,049
The third is the slow success.

93
00:05:25,139 --> 00:05:27,159
Everything completes eventually.

94
00:05:27,169 --> 00:05:33,499
Your check has a 30-second timeout, so a
request that takes 18 seconds is a pass.

95
00:05:33,759 --> 00:05:36,459
Your customer gave up at six.

96
00:05:36,970 --> 00:05:39,070
The fix isn't more checks.

97
00:05:39,309 --> 00:05:40,799
It's different checks.

98
00:05:40,819 --> 00:05:44,399
Instead of asking, "Did the
server respond?" You can ask,

99
00:05:44,709 --> 00:05:48,089
"Can a customer still complete
the thing they came here to do?"

100
00:05:48,599 --> 00:05:51,960
That means checking for the
actual content you expect on the

101
00:05:51,970 --> 00:05:54,539
page, not just the status code.

102
00:05:54,819 --> 00:05:59,819
It means calling the dependency directly
from the outside, the same way your

103
00:05:59,839 --> 00:06:05,239
app does, so you know it's degraded
before your app finds out, and it m-

104
00:06:05,639 --> 00:06:10,639
and it means setting the thresholds
on latency that match what a human

105
00:06:10,649 --> 00:06:13,569
will tolerate, not what a socket will.

106
00:06:13,679 --> 00:06:17,409
At Uptime Robot, this is the
difference between a plain HTTP

107
00:06:17,409 --> 00:06:22,570
check and a keyword or API check
that looks for the expected response.

108
00:06:22,749 --> 00:06:25,229
Same URL, completely different question.

109
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:28,679
Most teams don't actually
know what they depend on.

110
00:06:29,089 --> 00:06:30,569
Here's the uncomfortable part.

111
00:06:30,649 --> 00:06:34,619
Before you can monitor your dependencies,
you have to know what they are,

112
00:06:34,989 --> 00:06:39,169
and I've never met a team that
could list all of them from memory.

113
00:06:39,669 --> 00:06:44,049
Ask an engineering team what their
app depends on, and you'll get the big

114
00:06:44,119 --> 00:06:47,459
ones: payments, auth, the database.

115
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:50,969
Then someone remembers the email provider.

116
00:06:51,180 --> 00:06:56,480
Then someone else remembers the analytics
script that blocks page load if it's slow.

117
00:06:57,120 --> 00:07:02,920
Then there is the font CDN, the map
tiles, the feature flag service, the

118
00:07:02,920 --> 00:07:08,939
error tracker, and the AI API that a
contractor wrote in eight months ago

119
00:07:08,939 --> 00:07:11,739
and nobody has thought about it since.

120
00:07:11,789 --> 00:07:14,389
Dependencies hide in predictable places.

121
00:07:14,810 --> 00:07:18,669
Script tags and third-party
includes in your HTML.

122
00:07:19,109 --> 00:07:21,629
Outbound calls in your backend.

123
00:07:21,629 --> 00:07:27,459
DNS records, especially CNAMEs pointing
at someone else's infrastructure.

124
00:07:27,849 --> 00:07:30,200
TLS certificates issued to a vendor.

125
00:07:30,229 --> 00:07:31,679
Webhooks you receive.

126
00:07:32,070 --> 00:07:36,750
Environment variables full of API
keys to services nobody has audited.

127
00:07:37,259 --> 00:07:40,729
The practical move is to
go looking on purpose.

128
00:07:41,119 --> 00:07:45,469
Pull your homepage and a couple
of key pages and inventory

129
00:07:45,649 --> 00:07:48,419
every external host they load.

130
00:07:48,949 --> 00:07:53,689
Grab your code base for outbound
HTTP calls and API keys.

131
00:07:54,009 --> 00:07:55,639
Look at your DNS zone.

132
00:07:55,689 --> 00:08:01,429
Look at what your app talks to during
a real checkout, not just a page load.

133
00:08:01,790 --> 00:08:05,869
It's a couple of hours of
unglamorous work, and it is the

134
00:08:05,890 --> 00:08:10,029
single highest value monitoring
task most teams have never done.

135
00:08:10,220 --> 00:08:12,399
Once you have the list, group it.

136
00:08:12,659 --> 00:08:17,669
Payments, authentication, CDN
and DNS, email, analytics, AI.

137
00:08:18,029 --> 00:08:20,109
For each group, ask two questions.

138
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:25,569
If this degrades, what does a customer
experience, and would we know?

139
00:08:25,979 --> 00:08:29,839
For most teams, the honest answer
to the second question is, "We would

140
00:08:29,839 --> 00:08:34,839
find out from a support ticket."
Then monitor the ones where the

141
00:08:34,989 --> 00:08:37,039
answer to the first question hurts.

142
00:08:37,339 --> 00:08:40,679
You don't need to monitor the font CDN.

143
00:08:40,979 --> 00:08:44,779
You absolutely need to monitor
the thing that takes money.

144
00:08:44,979 --> 00:08:48,259
You can trust a platform
to report its own outage.

145
00:08:49,659 --> 00:08:55,329
Now, the objection I always get at this
point, our providers have status pages.

146
00:08:55,659 --> 00:08:58,079
If Stripe is down, Stripe will tell us.

147
00:08:58,239 --> 00:09:00,409
I want to be fair to status pages.

148
00:09:00,420 --> 00:09:04,339
They are useful, and most
vendors run them in good faith.

149
00:09:04,869 --> 00:09:07,269
But I'll make an argument
I'm happy to defend.

150
00:09:07,280 --> 00:09:11,459
You cannot rely on a platform to
report its own outage, and you

151
00:09:11,459 --> 00:09:15,009
shouldn't build your monitoring
on the assumption that it will.

152
00:09:15,149 --> 00:09:18,149
The reason is structural, not moral.

153
00:09:18,469 --> 00:09:21,679
A vendor status page is
updated by the vendor.

154
00:09:21,809 --> 00:09:27,800
The people updating it have a commercial
incentive to describe an incident as

155
00:09:27,849 --> 00:09:34,300
narrowly as possible, and an operational
ins- incentive to wait until they

156
00:09:34,300 --> 00:09:36,389
understand it before saying anything.

157
00:09:36,720 --> 00:09:38,639
So status pages lack.

158
00:09:39,020 --> 00:09:44,200
They describe degraded performance in
some regions while your checkout is dead.

159
00:09:44,510 --> 00:09:49,189
They resolve incidents before the tail
of the failure has actually cleared.

160
00:09:49,530 --> 00:09:55,179
And a partial degradation, the kind that
hurts you most, often never appear on

161
00:09:55,180 --> 00:09:59,959
a status page at all because from the
vendor's side, it's within tolerance.

162
00:10:00,019 --> 00:10:01,839
And this isn't a hunch.

163
00:10:01,839 --> 00:10:03,289
We have data on this.

164
00:10:03,289 --> 00:10:07,789
In the first half of this year,
looking only at services that do

165
00:10:07,799 --> 00:10:13,950
publish a status page, 89% of the
incidents users were reporting had

166
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:16,899
no official filling within six hours.

167
00:10:17,239 --> 00:10:23,169
And where the vendor did eventually file,
the crowd usually got there first by a

168
00:10:23,169 --> 00:10:29,094
median of about two hours Two hours is
a long time when it's your checkout.

169
00:10:29,094 --> 00:10:35,233
Even when everyone is acting honestly,
the status page is telling you about

170
00:10:35,233 --> 00:10:37,714
the vendor's experience of the incident.

171
00:10:38,054 --> 00:10:40,833
You need to know about
your experience of it.

172
00:10:41,123 --> 00:10:45,564
Those are different things, which
is why an outside observer matters

173
00:10:45,584 --> 00:10:50,153
and keeps mattering, no matter how
good your internal telemetry gets.

174
00:10:50,493 --> 00:10:54,993
Your internal metrics live
inside the system that's failing.

175
00:10:55,243 --> 00:10:58,493
Your provider's status page
lives inside the provider.

176
00:10:58,903 --> 00:11:03,354
The only thing that sees your product
the way your customer does is something

177
00:11:03,383 --> 00:11:08,854
standing outside both, making the
same requests a customer would from

178
00:11:08,883 --> 00:11:13,634
the places your customers are, and
telling you when the answer changes.

179
00:11:13,703 --> 00:11:17,793
That's the whole reason external
monitoring exists as a category,

180
00:11:18,023 --> 00:11:20,923
and it's the reason it doesn't
get replaced by better dashboards.

181
00:11:22,023 --> 00:11:24,543
A dashboard is a view from the inside.

182
00:11:24,813 --> 00:11:27,904
You need at least one
view from the outside.

183
00:11:27,953 --> 00:11:29,543
What to do this week?

184
00:11:29,644 --> 00:11:32,513
Let me make this concrete,
because practical takeaway

185
00:11:33,103 --> 00:11:34,253
is where the problem is.

186
00:11:34,744 --> 00:11:40,254
Start by spending two hours building the
dependency list, homepage, key pages,

187
00:11:40,293 --> 00:11:43,834
backend, outbound calls, DNS, API key.

188
00:11:43,863 --> 00:11:44,674
Write it down.

189
00:11:44,743 --> 00:11:45,914
You'll be surprised.

190
00:11:46,473 --> 00:11:51,213
Then, for every dependency where
failure costs money or locks people

191
00:11:51,253 --> 00:11:56,004
out, add an external check that
calls it the way your app does.

192
00:11:56,393 --> 00:12:01,493
Not a check on your page, a check
on the thing your page depends on.

193
00:12:01,623 --> 00:12:08,724
Next, upgrade your most important endpoint
checks from, "Did it return 200?" to, "Did

194
00:12:08,733 --> 00:12:16,813
it return what a customer needs?" Keyword
checks, API response validation, latency

195
00:12:17,713 --> 00:12:17,893
thresholds that match human patience.

196
00:12:17,893 --> 00:12:21,483
Put those checks somewhere
outside your own infrastructure

197
00:12:21,483 --> 00:12:23,173
and outside your vendor's.

198
00:12:23,423 --> 00:12:25,214
Independence is the point.

199
00:12:25,543 --> 00:12:29,694
And when a vendor status page
says all clear and your check says

200
00:12:29,713 --> 00:12:31,953
otherwise, believe your checks.

201
00:12:32,013 --> 00:12:33,243
That's what they are for.

202
00:12:33,313 --> 00:12:37,244
Your app being up and your business
being up are two different things,

203
00:12:37,513 --> 00:12:41,624
and modern architecture has pulled
them further apart than most

204
00:12:41,633 --> 00:12:44,064
monitoring setups have caught up with.

205
00:12:44,623 --> 00:12:48,743
I've been talking about this from
the Uptime Robot side, because it's

206
00:12:48,754 --> 00:12:54,564
what I see every day, but nothing
here depends on which tool you use.

207
00:12:54,943 --> 00:12:59,483
If you want to try it, the Uptime Robot
free plan gives you 50 monitors with no

208
00:12:59,483 --> 00:13:04,713
credit card, and it's enough to cover the
dependency list you are about to build.

209
00:13:05,123 --> 00:13:06,104
I'm Eleftheria.

210
00:13:06,223 --> 00:13:07,313
Thanks for watching.

211
00:13:07,333 --> 00:13:11,743
And if you've got a green dashboard
war story, I would love to hear it

